@implements IDisposable
@inject CharacterSession Session
@inject Theme Theme

@* The two palette switches, off the bar and behind one control.

   **This is what took the banner from five idioms to three.** The strip carried a wordmark, two
   underlined links, a bare button with two key boxes, a bordered pill and a second bordered pill
   with different horizontal padding — five ways of drawing a control, in a band about eleven
   centimetres wide. The owner reported it as "search is vertically elevated", which was true and
   was the smaller half: the reason nothing in the row lined up is that nothing in the row was the
   same kind of thing. Tuning the alignment of five idioms leaves five idioms.

   **So the two pills move in here rather than being made to match.** Neither is something a
   reader reaches for often — an identity is chosen once per character and a theme once per
   person — and both were sitting in the most valuable strip on the page, permanently, at the
   size of a primary control.

   **It completes a decision `docs/guide/browser.md` already records rather than reversing one.**
   "A rules search is not a Hero or a Villain" is why the subtitle names the palette in the
   builder and nowhere else; the switch that *sets* it was on every route regardless, which is
   the same fault one band up.

   **The disclosure mechanism is the character switcher's, deliberately.** That control already
   solved the one thing about hanging a menu off this band that is invisible to every rendering
   test: `view-transition-name` on `.banner` creates a stacking context, so a `z-index` on the
   menu itself is resolved *inside* the banner and the menu paints behind the step band. See the
   `z-index: 30` comment on `.banner` in app.css. A second mechanism here would have had to
   rediscover that in a screenshot. *@
<div class="settings-menu">
    @* aria-expanded and aria-pressed as strings: Blazor drops a false bool attribute and renders
       a true one as `aria-expanded=""`, which is invalid ARIA that assistive technology reads as
       *not* expanded — so the natural spelling announces the opposite of the state in both
       directions.

       aria-controls only while the list is actually rendered. Naming an element inside an @if
       unconditionally leaves a dangling IDREF, which is the bug the budget disclosure shipped
       once and `AriaReferenceTests` now sweeps for. *@
    <button type="button"
            class="banner-tool settings-open"
            aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
            aria-controls="@(_open ? "settings-menu-list" : null)"
            @onclick="Toggle">
        <span class="settings-open-label">Settings</span>@*
        The caret is the one thing that tells this control apart from the two beside it, which
        are the same face and size on purpose: Search opens an overlay, the account goes
        somewhere, and this drops a menu. Decoration rather than text — a screen reader is told
        the state by aria-expanded, and "Settings, down-pointing triangle" read aloud is noise.

        **Drawn in CSS rather than written as a glyph**, and that is the rule this app already
        lives by one band over: `Cmd` is spelled out rather than set as the looped-square glyph
        because that character is in neither typeface this app names and would fall back to a
        system face on one platform only, silently. A triangle character is the same bet. A
        border triangle names no family and cannot fall back.
    *@<span class="caret" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </button>

    @if (_open)
    {
        <div class="settings-menu-list" id="settings-menu-list">
            <p class="settings-menu-head">Palette</p>

            @* `.mode-switch` names the Hero/Villain control and not the pill shape. The
               light/dark control below briefly carried the same class, which made
               `.mode-switch button` match five buttons and the identity switch report three
               pressed states at once. `BannerTests` caught it in under a minute. *@
            <div class="mode-switch" role="group" aria-label="Hero or Villain">
                <button type="button"
                        aria-pressed="@Pressed(SheetMode.Hero)"
                        @onclick="() => SetMode(SheetMode.Hero)">Hero</button>
                <button type="button"
                        aria-pressed="@Pressed(SheetMode.Villain)"
                        @onclick="() => SetMode(SheetMode.Villain)">Villain</button>
            </div>

            <p class="settings-menu-head">Screen</p>

            @* **Three buttons rather than a two-state toggle**, because there are three states
               and the third is not "off": following the system is a live setting that changes
               with the machine, and a toggle can only ever land somebody on whichever value
               their system held at the moment they touched it. "Auto" is the word for it on the
               screen; `system` is the word everywhere else, and they are the same state. *@
            <div class="theme-switch" role="group" aria-label="Light or dark">
                <button type="button"
                        aria-pressed="@Chose(ThemeChoice.Light)"
                        @onclick="() => SetTheme(ThemeChoice.Light)">Light</button>
                <button type="button"
                        aria-pressed="@Chose(ThemeChoice.Dark)"
                        @onclick="() => SetTheme(ThemeChoice.Dark)">Dark</button>
                <button type="button"
                        aria-pressed="@Chose(ThemeChoice.System)"
                        @onclick="() => SetTheme(ThemeChoice.System)">Auto</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    private bool _open;

    /// <summary>Whether the identity switch shows this palette as the current one.</summary>
    private string Pressed(SheetMode mode) => Session.Mode == mode ? "true" : "false";

    /// <summary>Whether the light/dark control shows this choice as the current one.</summary>
    private string Chose(ThemeChoice choice) => Theme.Choice == choice ? "true" : "false";

    private void Toggle() => _open = !_open;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the light/dark control should show as current.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Read here rather than inherited from the layout, and the menu being shut is why.</b>
    /// The buttons are not in the document until somebody opens it, so nothing about the answer
    /// can wait on a render of theirs — it is asked once when this component initialises, which is
    /// on every route, and is a fact about the machine that cannot change while the tab is open
    /// except through the buttons themselves.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>The theme itself is already on the document by now: <c>js/theme.js</c> stamped it
    /// before the first paint, seconds before this ran. What is read here is only what the
    /// <em>control</em> should show, which is why nothing flickers when it arrives.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // The identity switch reports the character, which changes without this component being
        // touched — restored from storage, loaded as a sample, taken out of a recording. A menu
        // left open across one of those would otherwise show the palette of the character that
        // was on screen when it was opened.
        Session.Changed += Redraw;

        await Theme.ReadChoice();
    }

    public void Dispose() => Session.Changed -= Redraw;

    private void Redraw() => StateHasChanged();

    /// <summary>
    /// The Hero/Villain switch. It records the choice on the character and stops there.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It deliberately does not push the palette itself.</b> Setting the mode raises the
    /// session's change event, which redraws <see cref="Layout.MainLayout"/>, which applies the
    /// palette on the way out — the same path a restored character, a loaded sample and a
    /// character taken from a recording all take. Pushing here as well would be a second route to
    /// the same thing, and a second route is where the two get to disagree.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void SetMode(SheetMode mode) => Session.Mode = mode;

    /// <summary>
    /// The light/dark switch.
    ///
    /// <para>Unlike <see cref="SetMode"/> this does push to the document itself, and the
    /// difference is real rather than an inconsistency: the palette follows the <em>character</em>
    /// and so has to be applied on any render that changes which character is being built, while
    /// the theme follows the <em>reader</em> and changes on nothing but this click. There is one
    /// route into each and neither has two.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task SetTheme(ThemeChoice choice) => Theme.Choose(choice).AsTask();
}
